From 9645882c9a9b01423bb3a528cb72185725a466ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DeepSeek Harness <noreply@deepseek.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 02:40:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/18] WIC replaces ImageIO for emoticon images, and document
 the web view
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Emoticons.swift:203-214 re-encodes every downloaded picture to a PNG no larger
than 160 px on its longest side; the port had been caching the raw bytes under a
.png name, so a JPEG stayed a JPEG behind a lying extension, an animated GIF
kept every frame, and nothing capped the size. SmallPng now runs the picture
through IWICImagingFactory -> decoder -> GetFrame(0) -> format converter ->
scaler (only when a side exceeds 160, aspect kept, Fant) -> PNG encoder over a
memory stream. A picture already inside the box still goes through the encoder,
so a cache hit is always a real PNG.

One correction to the brief that changed the design, and it is worth recording:
net::Send's completion is posted to the MAIN thread, not to a net:: worker, so
decoding there would have put COM on OBS's UI thread and stalled it. The decode
and encode therefore run on one lazily started thread owned by liveclient.cpp,
with the answer posted back through dispatch::Post so the waiter map stays
main-thread-only. COM is initialised on that thread alone, S_FALSE is balanced
with CoUninitialize and RPC_E_CHANGED_MODE is tolerated without unbalancing. If
the thread cannot start, the callback caches the raw bytes synchronously —
the picture is never lost, and a container WIC cannot decode falls back the
same way with a comment saying so.

windowscodecs and shlwapi join the link line. livehime.dll is 4,132,352 bytes.

What is NOT claimed: this built and linked, but the runtime path did not
execute. The self-test's danmaku run sampled a quiet room this time (5 room
events, 0 emoticonImage) and no cache directory was created, so the encoder has
no observed output yet. Forcing it needs either a busier room or a direct
FetchEmoticonImage call in the self-test, and that is the next step rather than
something to assume.

CONTRACT.md §8.2 also stops calling the web view deferred, because it is not:
it records the SDK's provenance, the synchronous runtime probe, the three
things the page forced (both shims, the callNative reply, and the origin check
that WebView2's all-frames script injection makes load-bearing), and that
open_face_auth_qr is a native window rather than a web view.

